A||||8||Voyd||new Fiero owner, a few questions||||5
Z||000000||Voyd||10-29-1999||02:41 PM||||hello,<P>i just recently bought an '85 GT V6 Auto, and it is my first car. i got a very good deal on it, but, like everytime you get a good deal, there are certain things which need to be repaired.<P>i came to this forum hoping you guys could help me with a few questions i have:<P>1)when i immoblize the vehicule for more than 20 seconds, and the transmission is the Drive position, the engine cuts out. i see the RPM guage steadily going down, below 1000, and eventually to 500 and then a few sputters, and it cuts out. i can restart it no problem, but this keeps happening at virtually every stoplight. note that when i am in the Park position, it never cuts out.<P>2)the 4 tires that the car came with were cheap and worn out. since i am in the Great White North, winter is coming and i have alrady purchased some good winter tires. my question is what is the Stock tire size for an 85GT summer tire? <P>3) about brakes. i know that fieros dont exactly have the best braking in thw world, but how do you know if you need a brake job BAD? i mean, i know the brakes arent all that good right now, but whats a clear indication that they need to be changed? is in most cases changing only the brake pads good enough?<P>thanks for all responses im looking to absorb and learn all i can about these beautiful little cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||10-29-1999||03:15 PM||||1. likely the torque converter lockup solinoid is screwy. it's easily replaced. it's under the side cover on the tranny. Get the most recent kit from a dealer. It's been upgraded a few times over the years. You can verify it's the TCC lockup by unpluging the electrical connector on the front side of the tranny. If your problem stops, its the lockup. Don't just leave it unplugged. leaving it unplugged makes the tranny run hot.<P>2. The stock tire sizes vary a bit. the "correct ones" for your car are listed on the drivers door lable. you didn't tell us what size rims are on it, so we can't tell you too much.<P>3. brakes are shot when any of the following conditions are met.<BR>a. brake pad thickness below X. X varies a bit according to just what pads are on it. On rivited pads it's 1-2 32nths inch above the rivit heads.<BR>b. the rotors are warped. You can feel this in the peddal and/or steering wheel. suttle warp can be measured with a dial gauge.<BR>c. the rotors are below minimum thickness. this has to be measured in several places on the rotor. Discard rotors at or below min thickness.<P>Pads should not be replaced without turning the rotors. People do it without turning, but you can get some pretty wierd results. Rotors should be rechecked for thickness after each pass of turning. They must be check for thickness after final finishing. Don't use rotors below min thick. They can burst, crack, or otherwise fail very sudenly.<P>Caliper hardware must be inspected and replaced if damaged or worn. Caliper hardware must be properly lubricated during install or it can bind causing pad drag and/or poor braking. Calipers that leak, have binding pistons or e brake activator problems must be rebuilt or replaced.<P>You can improve Fiero braking considerably by using good pads. Don't go cheap on pads.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||10-29-1999||07:35 PM||||I have never owned an automatic Fiero so I'll take a called strike on the first problem.<P>All 1985 Fiero GT's came from the factory with 14x6 alloy wheels.  Stock tire size is 215/60R14.  You can sacrifice some dry grip and gain rain/snow traction by going with 195/70R14 - the exterior diameter is very close so the speedo should still be accurate.<P>Even in perfect condition, the 1984-87 brakes don't exhibit a super-firm pedal like the 1988's do.  Don't worry if it takes considerable leg effort to lock them.  But the pedal should firm up as you approach lockup.  If the pedal feels mushy to you, bleed the brakes.  It will only cost you a morning's work and might save your life.  And if you need pads, listen to the Ogre and spring for the best.  Enjoy your Fiero, Voyd - hope to see you at a show sometime!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||10-30-1999||06:34 PM||||My 88 doesn't brake too good. It has new pads but the driver rear rotor has a good bit of rust on it.  It still stops.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdm1964||10-30-1999||07:32 PM||||The rotor has rust on it because the caliper is not working, also the reason your brakes feel week. I would get that fixed right away. ||||reg||6
Z||000005||trackerrrr||10-30-1999||11:15 PM||||I agree with sdm1964, your caliper is frozen up or shot.  Fix this right away!  If there is one system on your Fiero that needs to work right every time it's the brakes!  It doesn't do any good to go fast if ya can't stop.||||reg||1
Z||000006||peterh||11-01-1999||08:59 AM||||What brand of brake pads are very good and where can I get them?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dave85||11-01-1999||05:42 PM||||Carbon Metallic are the best avalible.  Fiero Store has them for $37.90 set, front or rear. I've checked locally and I can't get them from anywhere around here.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-01-1999||06:11 PM||||Raybestos is also excelent. I think their best ones are carbomet. AutoZone and others have carbomets as well. Not all shops know what the pad composition is. Check manufacturer webs sites for info about specific product.||||reg||1
